        450.5508117675781,
        278.6593322753906,
        459.5072021484375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "In this section, we first introduce the data collection and filtering processes for both pre-training and",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        470.6463623046875,
        504.00140380859375,
        479.55291748046875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "SFT. We then describe how LLaDA leverages these datasets during training.",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        481.55535888671875,
        411.43084716796875,
        490.4619140625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Our pre-training corpus is constructed from diverse publicly available sources, including web data,",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        497.943359375,
        505.2470703125,
        506.84991455078125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "books, academic papers, social media, encyclopedias, mathematics, and code, with approximately",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        508.85235595703125,
        504.35223388671875,
        517.7589111328125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "11% Chinese, 61% English, and 28% code. The data cleaning process involves PDF text extraction,",
      "bbox": [
        107.25299835205078,
        519.7613525390625,
        505.2467346191406,
        528.6679077148438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "deduplication, and harmful content filtering. To further ensure quality, we fine-tune a BERT [22]",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        530.6703491210938,
        503.9999084472656,
        539.576904296875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "model for automated data quality annotation, enabling the selection of higher-quality samples. Our",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        541.5803833007812,
        504.1646728515625,
        550.4869384765625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "SFT dataset consists of 1 million human-annotated samples and 3.5 million synthetic samples,",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        552.4893798828125,
        505.24725341796875,
        561.3959350585938
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "generated using methods similar to those proposed in Xu et al. [103], Wei et al. [104].",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        563.3983764648438,
        450.0360107421875,
        572.304931640625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "We concatenate the collected documents in the pre-training corpus and segment the text into fixed-",
      "bbox": [
        107.53199768066406,
        579.786376953125,
        505.6533508300781,
        588.6929321289062
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "length sequences according to the predefined sequence length.",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        590.6953735351562,
        355.8993835449219,
        599.6019287109375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "For SFT, a dynamic sequence length strategy is employed, where |EOS| tokens are appended to the",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        606.3670654296875,
        504.0030822753906,
        615.9909057617188
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
